WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:08.170
Welcome

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.969
back to another video from ItsDrDan,
and this time we're going to be

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.560
doing an introduction to heat and
energy for introductory chemistry.

00:00:23.145 --> 00:00:27.235
So for whenever we have come
across heat and energy problems,

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:30.615
these can be extremely confusing,
especially the first time you were

00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:34.455
seeing them because they involve a
lot of simple algebraic equations.

00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:36.754
So let's try to break it down
to see what you're going to be

00:00:36.754 --> 00:00:38.164
seeing in these kinds of problems.

00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:41.444
So the first thing is, is
you're going to be coming across

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:43.695
heating and cooling curves.

00:00:44.075 --> 00:00:47.495
So a lot of times you're going to get
different types of diagrams that look

00:00:47.495 --> 00:00:50.714
similar to the ones that I have here,
where there's going to be temperature

00:00:50.715 --> 00:00:53.655
on one axis, energy on the other.

00:00:53.995 --> 00:00:58.765
So if something is being heated,
what we generally see that as is

00:00:58.795 --> 00:01:01.634
as a slope that is going upwards.

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:07.414
And if something is being cooled,
it is as a slope going downwards.

00:01:07.874 --> 00:01:11.285
So these are the two things
that we're going to be seeing.

00:01:11.285 --> 00:01:13.675
So we have cooling and heating.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:22.120
So whenever this is happening, the whole
idea with slopes is that the way that we

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:28.910
can do mathematical calculations regarding
these is using the MCAT equation.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.220
So it's q is equal to m c Delta T.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.330
So anyone that's a pre med out there
who might want to be a doctor one day,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.540
MCAT, it's a quick way to remember it.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.850
So what is all that
really broken down into?

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.329
What does all these
different symbols mean?

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.450
So for this one, it's all about change.

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:51.030
So slope is all about change.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.220
It's the same idea.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.860
So whenever we have Q,
that represents heat.

00:01:57.595 --> 00:02:03.215
And that's generally written in
the units of joules or calories.

00:02:03.615 --> 00:02:07.615
Very similar to the calories
that we ingest every day.

00:02:08.255 --> 00:02:11.285
We also have m for mass.

00:02:12.195 --> 00:02:14.635
That is going to be shown in grams.

00:02:15.345 --> 00:02:20.425
We have C, which C in this
case is for specific heat.

00:02:20.875 --> 00:02:28.225
Now, specific heat is for a specific
substance, meaning that it is unique

00:02:28.275 --> 00:02:31.865
for every single material that exists.

00:02:32.055 --> 00:02:34.559
Okay, so specific heat.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:40.950
So this is unique, so
I'll write that down.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.869
So, when it comes to specific heat,
the big thing with that one is it

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.750
reveals the units that are going
to be used in the entire equation.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.059
So this one is Joules,
grams, over Celsius.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.130
And then when it comes to delta
T, well, all right, well, what

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.480
does that all break down to?

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.410
Well, if you ever see a delta
symbol, that's telling you that

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.650
you have a change in something.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.670
And the way that that is calculated
is the final temperature.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.059
So wherever your final cooling spot is,
wherever your, your, uh, end point is.

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:18.320
minus your initial starting temperature.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.550
So if you're doing this in the lab, most
likely what you're going to be doing is

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.719
taking a thermometer, measuring something
at the very start of your experiment,

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:28.379
mixing some chemicals together, and then
you're going to see a temperature change.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:34.189
So from there, what kind of,
what other things might we see?

00:03:34.699 --> 00:03:39.779
So another type of representation
using these types of curves

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.640
is by showing plateaus.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.200
So if I have this heating curve
again, and I just want to show it by

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:55.630
a flat line, so a plateau, this is
representing that you are going through

00:03:55.709 --> 00:04:00.339
a phase change, or a phase transition.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.030
So what that means is that this
is something that requires a lot

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.015
of energy, And all it's doing is
going from maybe from a solid to

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.815
a liquid or a liquid to a gas.

00:04:12.225 --> 00:04:16.024
So, and those are represented
by very specific terminology.

00:04:16.414 --> 00:04:22.320
So if I'm going from a solid
To a liquid or vice versa.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:29.940
This is always in representation of
melting and freezing, but we're going

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:36.020
to be talking about things in a more
thermodynamic or heat sense meaning

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.960
that this is represented by Fusion
meaning we're bringing together or

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:47.030
bringing atoms closer together or slat
are starting to bring them apart So for

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:53.490
this one, it's also represented by heat
So this is a different version of Q.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:59.039
So, I'm going to label
it as Q, but, or as heat.

00:05:00.359 --> 00:05:08.320
And this is moles, so n for
moles, times delta H of fusion.

00:05:09.109 --> 00:05:12.889
Okay, and this one's generally
in kilojoules, is what that

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.090
one's going to be represented by.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:24.280
Now we also have when a liquid can
turn into a gas, so when that occurs,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.820
this is when we're vaporizing something
or we're turning it into particles.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.650
So when that's occurring, this is
gonna be another type of heat equation.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.490
Very similar.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:44.090
So it's Q heat and that's moles
times delta H of vaporization.

00:05:45.995 --> 00:05:49.975
And this one's occurring whenever
we have something that's boiling

00:05:50.575 --> 00:05:52.465
or going through condensation.

00:05:53.025 --> 00:05:58.714
So if you were kind of in class recently
and your teacher is going over all kinds

00:05:58.714 --> 00:06:03.524
of different parts of phase changes, this
is where we have to pay attention and

00:06:03.524 --> 00:06:09.895
see where are the temperatures rising to
our, is it going to cross a phase change?

00:06:10.245 --> 00:06:12.665
So what kind of problems
can we see with all this?

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.740
This is a lot of information
that we're going to kind of

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.640
start to see how we can apply it.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.560
So we have how many
different equations here?

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.919
We have two major or
three different equations.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.309
So let's kind of highlight
those for ourselves.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:27.830
So we have the MCAT equation.

00:06:27.879 --> 00:06:28.779
That is one of them.

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:30.020
Extremely important.

00:06:30.344 --> 00:06:31.275
Definitely write it down.

00:06:32.115 --> 00:06:37.905
Then you have your two, um, your two, uh,
different phase change equations, which

00:06:37.905 --> 00:06:42.324
are similar, but they're going to require
different constants that are applied.

00:06:42.324 --> 00:06:45.904
So Delta H is a constant
that will be given to you.

00:06:46.645 --> 00:06:47.185
All right.

00:06:47.275 --> 00:06:49.635
So I probably, I've made
a couple of questions.

00:06:49.645 --> 00:06:56.325
So the first one here is how much
heat is absorbed when 10 grams

00:06:56.344 --> 00:06:59.604
of water goes from 15 degrees C.

00:07:03.914 --> 00:07:10.534
So, being that this type of problem talks
about a phase change, or sorry not a

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:15.004
phase change, a change in temperature,
apologies, that means that we are

00:07:15.004 --> 00:07:18.175
going to be using the MCAT equation.

00:07:18.985 --> 00:07:23.815
And that's because in this problem, you
can see that we're changing temperature.

00:07:24.314 --> 00:07:28.874
So when we're doing this problem,
we want to write everything down.

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:32.584
So as we're kind of seeing,
going, looking at this problem,

00:07:33.044 --> 00:07:34.874
we can see a couple of key things.

00:07:34.904 --> 00:07:38.664
We see that, all right,
there's 10 grams of water.

00:07:39.614 --> 00:07:42.054
That is also a good part of information.

00:07:42.114 --> 00:07:43.124
This is water.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:48.300
Meaning we need to see what its
specific value is for specific heat.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:53.909
That's why I include the table to the left
and we have temperatures here as well.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.370
So we need to make sure we clearly label
what each of these values represent.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:03.730
So first thing is for MCAT
equation, we need mass.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.750
Mass in this problem is 10 grams.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:10.230
Now we have water.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.470
So C.

00:08:12.185 --> 00:08:16.445
It's gonna go off of this table
that I have on the left, and we can

00:08:16.445 --> 00:08:21.894
see water down here at the bottom,
and what that says is that's 4.

00:08:21.944 --> 00:08:22.334
184.

00:08:23.804 --> 00:08:28.455
Now, those units are written on
the top of that column, so that's

00:08:28.455 --> 00:08:32.395
joules over grams by Celsius.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.069
Now, what else do we have?

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.909
We have Ti, so your initial starting
point, and that mentions it's going

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:46.400
to start at 15 grams, or sorry,
not 15 grams, 15 degrees Celsius.

00:08:46.999 --> 00:08:49.030
And then we have final temperature.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.480
which is 50 degrees Celsius.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.060
The first thing we want to do
really quickly for ourselves to

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.870
help make our problem a little bit
easier is we can calculate what

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.340
our change in temperature is, which
is your final minus your initial.

00:09:05.919 --> 00:09:07.949
So we'll take those two values.

00:09:07.949 --> 00:09:12.800
So delta T is your final,
which is 50 degrees.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.810
Minus your initial, which is
50 degrees C, and that's going

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:22.520
to equal 35 degrees Celsius.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.449
Okay, now that is going to get
plugged into our MCAT equation.

00:09:27.449 --> 00:09:39.090
So what that's going to be is that
Q is equal to 10 grams times the 4.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:45.199
184 joules over grams by Celsius.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.819
So when we do that, we're going to
take all those values, plug them in.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.710
We're now going to put in our temperature,
which is 35 degrees C, and we're

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:57.710
going to multiply all these values.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.744
So we take 10, Times 4.

00:09:59.745 --> 00:10:03.224
184 times 35.

00:10:03.885 --> 00:10:08.665
So, when you get that, you should get
the following value from your calculator.

00:10:08.795 --> 00:10:12.225
You'll get 1, 464.

00:10:12.895 --> 00:10:13.705
4 joules.

00:10:15.525 --> 00:10:18.064
Now, is that a right
way to represent this?

00:10:18.074 --> 00:10:22.494
If you remember from your beginning days
of chem, significant figures, right?

00:10:22.565 --> 00:10:25.405
So that's always something that's
going to plague us for all eternity.

00:10:25.485 --> 00:10:27.775
It's something we're always going
to see throughout chemistry.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:31.420
So, in this problem, all your
starting values, you go off your

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:36.280
lowest one, in terms of sig figs,
and all of our starting ones are 3

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:38.990
and 4 sig figs, 3 being the lowest.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.010
So, we're going to round
our answer to 1460 joules.

00:10:45.295 --> 00:10:48.465
And that would be our
final answer for heat.

00:10:49.395 --> 00:10:55.665
Now, what if I ask you to convert
the previous answer to calories?

00:10:55.675 --> 00:11:00.245
So sometimes in these different problems,
you're going to be given, you're going

00:11:00.245 --> 00:11:04.574
to be told to make it in calories, which
is because it's a more relatable unit to

00:11:04.574 --> 00:11:08.165
the health sciences field, and which is
one of our biggest points of this channel

00:11:08.165 --> 00:11:09.895
is trying to help health science majors.

00:11:10.405 --> 00:11:11.705
So with that.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:18.780
What we will do is we'll take our
original value before we rounded it off.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:22.560
So that way we get the most
accurate representation of calories.

00:11:22.939 --> 00:11:26.640
And then we'll round that
calorie value to 3 sig figs.

00:11:27.639 --> 00:11:29.139
So, let's do it.

00:11:29.564 --> 00:11:33.615
So how, what is the conversion
from calories to joules?

00:11:33.645 --> 00:11:35.365
Well, it's actually a pretty easy one.

00:11:35.365 --> 00:11:38.295
It's the same thing as a
specific heat of water.

00:11:38.305 --> 00:11:40.574
A lot of our science is based on water.

00:11:41.745 --> 00:11:45.494
So, with this one, it's 4.

00:11:45.614 --> 00:11:49.394
184 joules is equal to 1 calorie.

00:11:50.145 --> 00:11:51.915
And that's a lowercase c.

00:11:52.675 --> 00:11:55.824
So, with that, we'll take the 464.

00:11:55.825 --> 00:11:57.615
4 joules.

00:11:58.545 --> 00:11:59.985
We'll write our t table.

00:12:02.235 --> 00:12:04.495
And now we've got to
cancel out our units, so 4.

00:12:04.495 --> 00:12:11.455
184 joules, we're going to show that
cancellation, if I can get this to

00:12:12.415 --> 00:12:17.345
cooperate, joules will cancel joules,
they are opposite each other, and then

00:12:17.345 --> 00:12:22.874
calories is on top, that's the only
thing that remains, so we take 1464.

00:12:22.875 --> 00:12:25.694
4 divided by 4.

00:12:25.695 --> 00:12:33.484
184 and we will get 350
calories as our answer.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.870
So, now we know how to do two things,
we know how to do slopes for heating,

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:41.310
which was our previous problem,
and now we can convert to calories.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.309
What other type of problems do you have?

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:46.670
A big one that you're kind of given
a lot when it comes to a lab based

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:50.859
course is you might be given an unknown
metal that you have to actually try

00:12:50.859 --> 00:12:52.769
to figure out what is it made out of.

00:12:53.189 --> 00:12:59.040
Now, being that every substance has
its own unique value to it, you can...

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.090
You can identify what it is.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:05.200
You can figure out if it's brass
or silver, gold, lead, you name it.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:09.479
The value exists in different tables
to figure out what it could be.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:18.310
So, if I have a sample of an unknown
metal with a mass of 125 grams, and

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:24.560
it rises in temperature by 10 degrees
Celsius when 481 joules of heat is

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.454
added, What element is the metal?

00:13:27.714 --> 00:13:33.415
So what we're trying to do is we're
trying to solve for C or specific heat.

00:13:33.925 --> 00:13:37.494
So when we have this equation,
we're going to be using our MCAT

00:13:37.495 --> 00:13:42.714
equation, but we're going to be
rearranging it to get C by itself.

00:13:43.224 --> 00:13:44.824
So what's the easiest way to do that?

00:13:44.865 --> 00:13:52.275
Well, if I divide both sides by m
and delta t, we'll have a couple

00:13:52.285 --> 00:13:53.795
things that will cancel out.

00:13:54.185 --> 00:14:00.385
m will cancel and then delta t
will cancel, leaving c by itself.

00:14:01.364 --> 00:14:08.574
So what this equation is going to turn
into is c is equal to q m delta t.

00:14:09.375 --> 00:14:11.695
So now we can plug in all of our values.

00:14:11.695 --> 00:14:18.235
So our q, in this case,
Was the 481 joules.

00:14:19.515 --> 00:14:29.015
We had a mass, which was 125 grams, and
then we had a delta T of 10 degrees C.

00:14:30.024 --> 00:14:36.455
Now, when we plug all that in, so we
do 481, and we divide by 125 times 10

00:14:36.515 --> 00:14:40.464
in parenthesis, and you should get 0.

00:14:40.584 --> 00:14:50.564
385 joules over grams by Celsius, which
is your units of, of, of specific heat.

00:14:51.084 --> 00:14:55.555
So now when we have that value,
now our, our big thing is, is try

00:14:55.555 --> 00:14:56.775
to figure out what metal is it?

00:14:56.775 --> 00:14:57.854
Well, how do I do that?

00:14:57.854 --> 00:14:59.614
Well, hopefully you're given a table.

00:14:59.614 --> 00:15:02.055
If not, you might need, you
need to look in your textbook.

00:15:02.074 --> 00:15:03.964
There's usually tables at the end of them.

00:15:04.524 --> 00:15:06.555
So, but for us, we have
a little table here.

00:15:06.775 --> 00:15:08.365
So we had 0.

00:15:08.365 --> 00:15:08.674
385.

00:15:08.854 --> 00:15:13.319
So with that, you can see that, oh,
That matches the value of copper.

00:15:13.319 --> 00:15:21.459
So our experiment showed that we have
copper as our unknown metal, which is

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.339
great, that's exactly what we want.

00:15:24.369 --> 00:15:27.890
Now, what's the hardest
type of problem you can see?

00:15:28.459 --> 00:15:29.359
Let's go through it.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:35.959
So whenever you're given a problem,
sometimes you might have a type of problem

00:15:35.959 --> 00:15:41.169
that goes through many different phase
changes and heating, meaning it's putting

00:15:41.169 --> 00:15:43.660
it all together in one super problem.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.130
So what does that mean?

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:47.030
What is it going to look like?

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:48.060
So here's an example.

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.630
If I ask you, how much heat in kilojoules?

00:15:53.195 --> 00:15:59.565
is required to turn 100 grams
of ice at negative 100 degrees

00:15:59.575 --> 00:16:03.615
C to steam at 250 degrees C.

00:16:04.675 --> 00:16:06.295
Why the heck do you even calculate that?

00:16:06.295 --> 00:16:08.605
So I give a bunch of C values for it.

00:16:08.904 --> 00:16:15.055
Notice how the specific heat
changes from ice to water to steam.

00:16:15.685 --> 00:16:21.945
So one huge tip for any heating,
cooling, phase change problem is you

00:16:21.945 --> 00:16:28.330
want to go back through it And try
to diagram it out using slopes and

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:33.679
plateaus so that you can see what
calculations you need to complete.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:37.419
Because for every slope,
meaning every point between a

00:16:37.419 --> 00:16:39.910
transition, use the MCAT equation.

00:16:40.659 --> 00:16:44.090
For every flat point, you're going
to be using either the fusion

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:45.960
or the vaporization formula.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.079
So, let's map out R's.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:53.320
So for this one, it says that we
start at negative 100 degrees C.

00:16:53.359 --> 00:16:54.500
That's really cold.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.219
So what we have to do is remember
what the freezing point and

00:16:58.219 --> 00:17:00.650
the boiling point is of water.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:04.909
So for us, I put down here
the the melting point here.

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:08.690
So the melting point for
water is 0 degrees C.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:13.549
So our original temperature is
going to be starting somewhere

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.940
around 100, negative 100 degrees.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.819
So it's really cold, so negative 100.

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.760
And what that's going to be
doing is it's going to be

